<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Healing Plants of Panama</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Soothing Guanabana</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow extended speech on familiar topics. Interactive: Ensure mutual understanding in group interactions through clarification and confirmation.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow spoken explanations about herbal remedies (e.g., "How do we prepare it"). Can clarify understanding of instructions about plant uses (e.g., "Can you repeat how to use the plant?"). Can interpret peer feedback on how to use healing plants by summarizing key points (e.g., "Juan Carlos said that guanabana (soursop) helps with digestion.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows large, clear pictures of common Panamanian healing plants like guanabana (soursop), sábila (aloe vera), hierba de limón (lemongrass), and menta (mint). Students look at the pictures. The teacher asks, "Do you know these plants?" "Have you seen them?" "Do they grow here in Panama?" (Students respond with short answers or gestures). The teacher points to a picture of guanabana and asks, "What do you think this plant is used for?" to activate prior knowledge. (3-5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of guanabana and says clearly, "This is guanabana, or soursop. It's a very useful plant. It's used to treat infections." The teacher writes 'guanabana' on the board. Then, the teacher holds up a picture of sábila and says, "This is aloe vera, or sábila. It's used to soothe burns. You should use it for skin problems." The teacher models the pronunciation of 'soursop', 'therapeutic', 'treatment', emphasizing the first syllable of compound nouns. (5-7 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modeled language and concepts.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is guanabana used for cooking or for health?" (Expected: For health.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you have a burn, should you use guanabana or aloe vera?" (Expected: Aloe vera.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'therapeutic' a good thing or a bad thing?" (Expected: A good thing.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students listen to a short audio clip (approximately 2 minutes) titled "Panama's Natural Remedies." The audio describes the traditional uses of soursop (guanabana), aloe vera (sábila), and mint leaves (menta) for common ailments, explicitly using "used to + infinitive verb" and "should/shouldn't."
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Genre:</b> Informative audio segment/description.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Register:</b> Clear, slightly formal, instructional.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Format:</b> Recorded narration with calm background music.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening for specific information about health remedies and understanding advice. Applying syllable division rules to decode words like 'treatment' and 'therapeutic' heard in the audio.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listen (Gist):</b> Students listen once and answer: "What is the audio mainly about? A) Recipes, B) Panamanian healing plants, C) Exercising."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listen (True/False):</b> Students listen again and decide if the following statements are True or False.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Soursop is used to treat infections." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You should boil aloe vera for burns." (False – applies directly)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Mint leaves are good for stomach aches." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listen (Matching):</b> Students listen one more time and match the plant to its primary use as mentioned in the audio.
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">1. Guanabana (Soursop) --- a. Soothe burns</p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2. Sábila (Aloe Vera) --- b. Treat infections</p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3. Menta (Mint leaves) --- c. Relieve headaches</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Receptive listening for details, clarification in interaction. Using grammar: Infinitive of purpose (used to + infinitive verb), Should/shouldn't for giving advice, Present simple for facts. Vocabulary: plant, leaf, root, remedy, ginger, infections, treatment, herb, application, soursop, aloe vera, lemongrass, mint leaves, treat, heal, soothe, apply, grow, boil, prepare, useful, medicinal, natural, therapeutic. Pronunciation of compound nouns (herbal garden) and syllable division (treatment, therapeutic).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listening Gap-fill:</b> Students receive a handout with a partial transcript of the audio. They listen to the audio again (or a specific segment) and fill in the blanks with the missing vocabulary words (e.g., 'remedy', 'heal', 'application') or grammatical structures (e.g., 'used to', 'should'). (e.g., "Guanabana is ______ ______ treat infections.") (10 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Complete (Sentence Builders):</b> The teacher reads the beginning of sentences related to the audio, and students complete them orally, ensuring they use the target structures and vocabulary accurately. For example, Teacher: "Aloe vera is..." Students: "...used to soothe burns." Teacher: "If you have a headache, you should..." Students: "...use mint leaves." The teacher pauses to allow students to listen to each other's responses and self-correct. (8 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Checking & Clarification:</b> In pairs, students take turns reading aloud one of the completed sentences from activity 2. Their partner listens carefully and must confirm understanding or ask for clarification.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Soursop is used to treat infections."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Did you say 'soursop is used to treat infections'?" or "Can you repeat how to use the soursop, please?" (Focus on active listening and interaction to ensure mutual understanding.) (7 min)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task using language in scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Herbal Remedy Advisory Circle." Students work in small groups of 3-4. One student acts as the "patient" and describes a simple, common ailment (e.g., "I have a small cut," "I have a stomachache," "I feel very tired"). The other students act as "advisors" and take turns listening to the "patient" and then suggesting a specific healing plant from Panama and how it's used, providing advice using "should/shouldn't" or stating purpose with "used to."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly places students in the scenario of "The Healing Plants of Panama," using "The Soothing Guanabana" and other plants as solutions to health problems. It encourages authentic listening for needs and providing helpful advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Students actively listen to understand the ailment, then formulate and deliver advice. The "patient" practices interactive listening by asking for clarification if the advice is unclear (e.g., "Can you repeat how to prepare the remedy?"). Other group members listen to all suggestions and collaboratively confirm understanding. One designated listener in each group has to summarize the advice given to the "patient."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment and self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Herbal Remedy Advisory Circle" activity. The teacher circulates, noting down students' ability to listen, clarify, and understand. Additionally, a short Exit Ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> As part of the "Herbal Remedy Advisory Circle," each "patient" student is asked to briefly summarize the advice they received from their group. For example, "Juan Carlos said that guanabana (soursop) helps with digestion," or "Maria suggested that I should use aloe vera for my cut." This demonstrates their listening comprehension and ability to interpret peer feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> (e.g., clear articulation of 'treatment', 'therapeutic', 'soursop').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., 'plant', 'remedy', 'treat', 'soothe', 'application').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity in understanding:</b> Can follow spoken explanations and advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration/Interaction:</b> Engages in clarification and confirmation to ensure mutual understanding.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two short reflective prompts on a slip of paper before leaving.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one healing plant you learned about today and one thing it's used for."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to understand your classmates' advice today? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Review, self-assessment, and future planning)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting fact you heard about the healing plants today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase you learned about using plants for health, and how can you remember it?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in terms of engaging students and focusing on listening comprehension?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students effectively use clarification strategies (e.g., "Can you repeat...?") when listening to their peers and the audio?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds might be needed for students struggling to follow extended speech or use the target grammar? (e.g., providing more visual aids, simpler audio scripts, or sentence starters for advice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students grasp the pronunciation of compound nouns and syllable division?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In this lesson, we listened to descriptions and advice about the healing plants of Panama, focusing on guanabana and other remedies. We learned what they are 'used to' and how we 'should' use them. Next, in Lesson 2, we will read short texts and articles about these *same* healing plants to further deepen our understanding and identify key information about their properties and traditional preparations."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>